22:18:45 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A country can have social norms that differ from a Western office culture, but an article about broad cultural tendencies should not be used to explain or predict one colleague’s performance. Eritrea is diverse, and “pace of life” outside work is not the same as reliability, professionalism, or willingness to meet workplace commitments. You can acknowledge that people may have different assumptions about planning, hierarchy, or how firmly deadlines are communicated—without assuming that’s what is happening here. The practical response remains the same: - State deadlines explicitly, including time zone and whether they are firm. - Explain why the deadline matters and what downstream work depends on it. - Agree on interim milestones and check-ins. - Ask her directly whether workload, unclear priorities, approvals, tools, or expectations are creating problems. - Confirm the plan in writing. - Hold her to the same reasonable standards and support process as everyone else. For example: “For this report, the final version needs to be in the shared folder by 3 p.m. Thursday because Finance needs it Friday morning. Could you send me an outline Monday and a draft Wednesday? If that timeline won’t work, please tell me by today so we can adjust scope or get help.” That is clearer and more respectful than trying to infer the cause from nationality.
