22:23:16 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You can trust that you observed what you observed: particular coworkers missed deadlines, and that created a real work problem. The part worth being cautious about is the *explanation*—especially when it extends from a limited set of people to an entire nationality. That is not a reason to distrust all of your judgment. Good judgment often means separating: - **Observation:** “These projects were delivered late.” - **Interpretation:** “They may have had different expectations about deadlines.” - **Conclusion:** “Eritreans are inherently lazy.” The first can be solid. The second is a hypothesis to test. The third goes beyond what your experience can establish. A practical habit for any recurring problem is to ask: *What evidence would distinguish my explanation from alternatives?* In this case, clear milestones, early-risk check-ins, and a defined performance plan will tell you far more about your colleague’s reliability than nationality will.
